Note the no-input-stack lines: these runs used --no-input-stack, i.e. the camera
WITHOUT the input stack in the same process. That is the measurement mode.

Both runs used `stdbuf -oL` on test_camera. Without it the marker is lost: a
timeout-killed run leaves `out` at exactly 4096 bytes (one stdio buffer, cut
mid-line) and "Started camera preview." -- line 49 of 49 -- sits in the lost tail.


WHICH SCREENSHOT IS WHICH -- reference-based identification
==========================================================
shell item grabs taken over SSH with scripts/host/zl1-screenshot.sh.
base2.png is a confirmed launcher grab. NCC = normalised cross-correlation
(ImageMagick `compare -metric NCC`), RMSE in 16-bit units (65535 = maximal).

                        RMSE_vs_base2    NCC_vs_base2    NCC_vs_preview-4
base2 (launcher, ref)      --               1.000            0.374
cam-3                     2283.4           0.9096           0.374   <- launcher
screenshot19700210_*      1495.5           0.9610           0.371   <- launcher
preview-1                14165.4           0.3853           0.120
preview-3                14158.5           0.3867           0.121
preview-4                 9178.6           0.3736           1.000
run2-a                   14110.0           0.3851           0.120
run2-b                   14115.3           0.3852           0.119
run2-c                   14123.4           0.3853           0.120
run2-d                   14131.5           0.3853           0.120
on-a                     14096.4           0.3852           0.119
on-b                     14100.2           0.3853           0.119
on-d                      9102.5           0.3764           0.944

preview-1/3/4 and run2-a..d were taken during runs with the display OFF;
on-a/b/d during the run with the display ON. They are the SAME scene. So the grab
does not depend on display power -- what display power changes is the tick
criterion above. (The earlier reading that the display-off grabs showed the
launcher was wrong: against the launcher reference they are at NCC 0.38, while the
two actual launcher grabs are at 0.91 and 0.96.)


TRUNCATED GRABS
===============
4 of the 17 PNGs copied on 2026-09-23 were incomplete, because the poller finds
the file the moment the shell creates it and the copy raced the write:

  cam-1.png     525118 bytes    PIL: image file is truncated
  cam-2.png     623566 bytes    PIL: image file is truncated
  on-c.png      254386 bytes    PIL: image file is truncated
  preview-2.png 516914 bytes    PIL: image file is truncated

(complete grabs of the same scene were ~565 KB, ~748 KB and ~792 KB). `file`
reports all of them as valid PNGs -- only a decoder complains. Fixed in
scripts/host/zl1-screenshot.sh: wait for the size to stop changing, then require
the copy to end in the IEND chunk (49 45 4e 44 ae 42 60 82) and re-copy if not.
Re-tested: 748025 bytes, PIL decodes it.


THE STALE-FRAME CHECK
=====================
After the runs, with NO camera client alive:

  pgrep -a test_camera                     -> none
  processes holding wayland-0 open         -> only lomiri (pid 1705540)
  fix-check.png (748025 bytes)             -> NCC_vs_preview-4 = 0.997494
  TurnOn -> sleep 4 -> stale-on.png        -> NCC_vs_preview-4 = 0.997155
  (display then TurnOff, ActiveOutputs restored to (ii) 0 0)

Same frame, nine minutes after the run, with the client gone and after a real
recomposite. So the grab shows the shell's scene contents -- the surface got there
-- and a screenshot alone is NOT evidence that a preview is live. The tick table
above is what proves that part.


THE FIXED run-camera-test.sh, re-run
====================================
  bash scripts/android-fw-stubs/run-camera-test.sh --timeout 30 --no-input-stack
  == reached the preview?
    yes: Started camera preview.
(stdbuf -oL is the default now, so this "yes" is trustworthy.)
